    position: -20 2.8 20
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:35affda647ed441593518eb867ab6cee#c1bfa30a, material: vx:a39d681a76b0447d8a267c219693c170, castsShadows: true }
      - !Light { kind: Point, unit: Lumen, intensity: 140000, temperature: 2200, range: 26, radius: 0.14 }
      - !VfxEmitter { effect: vx:611abda7a814472b9618b8eda16e61b6, playing: true, reach: 4.0, rise: 1.6 }
  - name: Lamp14
    position: 20 2.8 -20
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:35affda647ed441593518eb867ab6cee#c1bfa30a, material: vx:a39d681a76b0447d8a267c219693c170, castsShadows: true }
      - !Light { kind: Point, unit: Lumen, intensity: 140000, temperature: 2200, range: 26, radius: 0.14 }
      - !VfxEmitter { effect: vx:611abda7a814472b9618b8eda16e61b6, playing: true, reach: 4.0, rise: 1.6 }

  # The lake, out through the north gate. Two entities, because a zone and a body are two things:
  # the zone is the window the field is rasterised into and the body is one piece of water in it.
  #
  # ⚠ **A zone must exist or nothing renders**, which is Unreal's rule kept for its reason: the field
  # is the interchange every consumer reads, and a body with no zone is a body nothing has rasterised.
  # `WaterZoneSystem.ZonelessBodies` is the number that says so, and `Arena.ReportFrame` prints it.
  #
  # ⚠ **The zone's window follows the *view*, not this entity.** A zone entity's transform places it
  # in the hierarchy for prefabs and duplication and decides nothing about which texels are covered —
  # doc 35 § D3 makes the window a sliding one, so the 256 m square is centred wherever the camera is.
  # Which is why this is at the origin and the *body* is at the lake.
  - name: LakeZone
    position: 0 0 0
    components:
      - !WaterZoneComponent
        # 256 m over 257 samples is exactly one metre a texel — the resolution has to be a power of
        # two *plus one*, because the samples include both edges, and 256 would be 1.0039 m a texel
        # against a snap grid stated in whole metres, which is a shoreline that crawls as you walk.
        extent: 256
        resolution: 257
        precision: Full
        scrollThreshold: 0.25
        # The bed is 3.8 m below the surface at the middle, so the waves are at full height there and
        # attenuate to nothing over the last four metres of the shelf. Attenuation lives in the
        # evaluator rather than the material, so the buoyancy solver and the vertex stage agree.
        attenuationDepth: 4
        waveAsset: Assets/Water/Lake.vxwaves
  - name: Lake
    # ⚠ `TerrainSeed.LakeCentre`, and the two have to match: this is where the *surface* is and that
    # is where the *bed* is, and depth is their difference. They are in two files because one is a
    # committed heightfield and the other is a scene; nothing in the build compares them, so
    # `FrameDocumentTests.The_lake_in_the_scene_sits_over_the_bed_the_seed_dug` does.
    position: 0 0 -62
    components:
      - !WaterBodyComponent
        kind: Lake
        spline: Assets/Water/Lake.vxspline
        # TerrainSeed.LakeSurface. Forty centimetres below the rim the bed rises to, so the water
        # runs out inside the dug bowl rather than exactly at its edge.
        surfaceHeight: 1.2
        priority: 1
        shoreFalloff: 1.5
        bedRamp: 6
        depth: 4.2

  # A raft on it, and it is the first floating body in any game in this tree.
  #
  # ⚠ **`BuoyancySystem` is opted into by a game — doc 35 § D1 — and until this entity existed
  # nothing opted in.** Every `new BuoyancySystem(…)` in the repository was a test, so the solver
  # was correct against an analytic waterline and had never once been asked to float anything a
  # person could look at. `Arena.Register` is the opt-in; this is what it has to work on.
  #
  # ⚠ **The pontoons are a displacement volume and not the deck.** Four spheres of 0.45 m at the
  # corners of a 2.4 × 4 m deck, written *into the file* because that is the half that had no
  # coverage in a shipped scene: `BuoyancyPontoon` needed its own `[DataContract]` and shipped
  # without one, and a body with no pontoons floats nowhere and is *not an error* — so an authored
  # boat loaded, looked complete in the inspector, and sank.
  #
  # ⚠ **The mass is what puts the waterline where it is, and it was solved rather than tuned.**
  # Four spheres of r = 0.45 hold 4 × 4/3πr³ = 1.527 m³. At Archimedes exactly — `coefficient: 1`,
  # so nothing here is a fudge — a mass of 640 kg displaces 0.640 m³, which is 0.419 of the
  # spheres, which by the spherical cap is a cap 0.401 m deep. The centres therefore rest 0.049 m
  # *above* the surface and the deck's base, 0.2 m below them, rests 0.151 m under it: a 0.4 m deck
  # cut roughly in half by the water. Against `surfaceHeight: 1.2` that is a deck base at y ≈ 1.05.
  # 640 kg for a 2.4 × 4 m hollow pontoon deck is a real number, not one chosen to make a picture.
  #
  # ⚠ **`scale` moves the mesh and does not move the collider**, the same hand-kept pair every
  # other entity here has: the crate mesh is a 1.6 m cube with its origin at its *base*, so the
  # scale is the wanted size over 1.6 and `!BoxCollision` says the same box in metres — with
  # `centre` lifting it half a deck, because the mesh's origin is at the base and the shape's is at
  # its middle. The pontoons are in that same base-origin frame, which is why their y is 0.2.
  - name: Raft
    # Inside `TerrainSeed.LakeRadius` and towards the near bank, so that it is in shot from ground a
    # character can stand on: 12.2 m from `LakeCentre` is 1.9 m of water, which is deep enough for
    # the swell to be half its open-water height and shallow enough that the shore is a short swim.
    #
    # ⚠ **0.95 m above where it will end up, and dropped rather than placed.** A body *arriving* at
    # its waterline is evidence the solver did it; a body authored already at rest is evidence of
    # nothing at all, and is exactly what a run with the system unregistered would also look like.
    position: 7 2 -52
    scale: 1.5 0.25 2.5
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:ce6f44a9d82149d3bf50c6d30381b440, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 1.2 0.2 2, centre: 0 0.2 0, friction: 0.4 }
      # ⚠ Every field written out, and none of them is decoration. `RigidBody` has no default
      # component value, so a zeroed one is `Motion: Static` (0), `gravityFactor: 0` and
      # `degreesOfFreedom: None` (0) — three separate ways to author a raft that hangs in the air
      # over a lake with the whole buoyancy path running and every counter non-zero.
      - !RigidBody
        motion: Dynamic
        motionQuality: Discrete
        mass: 640
        gravityFactor: 1
        linearDamping: 0
        angularDamping: 0
        degreesOfFreedom: All
        # ⚠ Not allowed to sleep. A hull that settles is a hull Jolt deactivates, and a deactivated
        # body stops integrating the forces this system is still applying — so the raft would stop
        # riding the swell a couple of seconds in and sit at a fixed height on moving water.
        allowSleeping: false
      - !BuoyancyBody
        pontoons:
          - offset: -0.9 0.2 -1.5
            radius: 0.45
          - offset: 0.9 0.2 -1.5
            radius: 0.45
          - offset: 0.9 0.2 1.5
            radius: 0.45
          - offset: -0.9 0.2 1.5
            radius: 0.45
        coefficient: 1
        # ⚠ Not zero, and it is the one zero `BuoyancyBody.Settings` does not rescue. An unset
        # coefficient becomes one there; unset damping stays zero and is a hull that oscillates on
        # the surface for ever, which reads as the solver being wrong rather than as a blank field.
        damping: 3
        quadraticDamping: 0.4
        flowDrag: 2
      - !BuoyancyState
        wet: 0

  # Where players come in. Found by component, because a compiled scene carries no name table
  # a running game can query — see SpawnPoint.
  - name: Spawn0
    position: -20 0.2 -20
    rotation: 0 0.382683 0 0.923880
    components:
      - !SpawnPoint { index: 0 }
  - name: Spawn1
    position: 20 0.2 -20
    rotation: 0 -0.382683 0 0.923880
    components:
      - !SpawnPoint { index: 1 }
  - name: Spawn2
    position: -20 0.2 20
    rotation: 0 0.923880 0 0.382683
    components:
      - !SpawnPoint { index: 2 }
  - name: Spawn3
    position: 20 0.2 20
    rotation: 0 -0.923880 0 0.382683
    components:
      - !SpawnPoint { index: 3 }


  # ── somewhere with an inside ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Three boxes with doorways, because every measurement this level makes is an outdoor one and
  # half the things the frame does only show indoors. A closed room is where ambient occlusion has
  # a corner to darken, where the exposure meter has something to adapt *to*, and where a
  # post-process volume has a threshold to cross.
  #
  # ⚠ **`scale` moves the mesh and does not move the collider.** `BoxCollision.halfExtents` is read
  # straight into a shape by `Arena.BuildCollision`, which never looks at the transform — so the two
  # numbers on each entity below describe the same box in two different units and have to be kept
  # in step by hand. The mesh is the 1.6 m crate cube with its origin at its base, so a scale is the
  # wanted size over 1.6 and the half-extents are the wanted size over two.
  #
  # ⚠ Each bulb is 1 400 lm at 2 700 K — a domestic fitting, and a thousandth of what the sodium
  # floodlights outside deliver. That is the correct number and it is *why* these rooms are worth
  # having: outdoors it would be invisible, and indoors it is the only light there is.

  # HouseA — a 8 m box with a 1.8 m doorway on its south side.
  - name: HouseAWallN
    position: -14 0 4
    scale: 5.0000 2.0000 0.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 4 1.6 0.15, centre: 0 1.6 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseAWallS0
    position: -16.45 0 12
    scale: 1.9375 2.0000 0.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 1.55 1.6 0.15, centre: 0 1.6 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseAWallS1
    position: -11.55 0 12
    scale: 1.9375 2.0000 0.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 1.55 1.6 0.15, centre: 0 1.6 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseALintel
    position: -14 2.2 12
    scale: 1.1250 0.6250 0.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 0.9 0.5 0.15, centre: 0 0.5 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseAWallW
    position: -18 0 8
    scale: 0.1875 2.0000 5.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 0.15 1.6 4.15, centre: 0 1.6 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseAWallE
    position: -10 0 8
    scale: 0.1875 2.0000 5.1875
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:60d8f50a41fb405eb427664245340ba0, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 0.15 1.6 4.15, centre: 0 1.6 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseARoof
    position: -14 3.2 8
    scale: 5.3750 0.1875 5.3750
    components:
      - !MeshRenderable { mesh: vx:37b8ae38d99d4be19b8b772f06d08e05#c9030bac, material: vx:dfb64c6f6a7b4fe5a33d5626e0f73720, castsShadows: true }
      - !BoxCollision { halfExtents: 4.3 0.15 4.3, centre: 0 0.15 0, friction: 0.5 }
  - name: HouseABulb
    position: -14 2.75 8
    components:
      - !Light { kind: Point, unit: Lumen, intensity: 1400, temperature: 2700, range: 8, radius: 0.05 }
      - !VfxEmitter { effect: vx:611abda7a814472b9618b8eda16e61b6, playing: true, reach: 4.0, rise: 1.6 }

  # HouseB — a 7 m box with a 1.8 m doorway on its south side.
